                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h3#equivariant Equivariant
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#MooreSegal06"}> Moore-Segal 06
            ) are discussed
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                equivariant TFT
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/equivariant+TFT"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+G-algebras"} crossed G-algebras
            (which they call Turaev algebras).
          %h2#history_2 History
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  elliptic objects
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/elliptic+objects"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Segal88"} Segal 88
          %blockquote
            %p 6. Speculation about the definition of elliptic cohomology
            %p
              For any space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_X
              be the category whose objects are the points of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and whose morphisms from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              are the paths in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_X
              to finite dimensional vector spaces is essentially the same thing as a vector bundle on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with a connection. (The functor must be continuous in a suitable sense.) It is well known how K-theory is constructed from such objects.
            %p
              I have described elsewhere [23] a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              whose objects are all compact oriented one-dimensional manifolds, and whose morphisms from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_0
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
              are pairs
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma,\alpha)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a Riemann surface with boundary
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial\Sigma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is an isomorphism between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial X
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1-S_0
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma,\alpha)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma',\alpha')
              are identified if they are isomorphic. For any space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              one can now define a category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,s)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is an object of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \colon S \to X
              is a map. Its morphisms from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S_0,s_0)
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S_1,s_1)
              are triples
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi α
                      %mo ,
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma,\alpha,\sigma)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma,\alpha)\colon S_0 \to S_1
              is a morphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\colon\Sigma\to X
              is a map compatible with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi O
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s_O,s_1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_X
              is a natural analogue of the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_X
              which gives rise to vector bundles.
            %p
              It is appropriate to consider functors from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              to the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}
              of topological vector spaces and trace-class maps. If such a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is holomorphic in the natural sense then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(S^1)
              is a positive energy representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Diff
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Diff(S^1)
              of finite type. More precisely, as is familiar in the representation theory of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Diff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Diff(S^1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              of some definite positive integral level
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              ensures that the character of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(S^1)
              is a modular form of weight
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %p
              Now let us define an elliptic object of level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              as a projective functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒱
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\colon\mathcal{C}_X\to \mathcal{V}
              of level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              which is holomorphic and satisfies the contraction condition. Such an object consists of an infinite dimensional vector bundle on the loop space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Diff
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Diff(S^1)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a spin manifold with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1=0
            %p
              I have nothing precise to say about elliptic objects, but it seems to me quite likely that the objects of each level lead to an interesting cohomology theory, and that the theories for different levels are related by “Bott maps”. That would fit in well with Theorem (5.3), for just as elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(B G)
              are elated to flat bundles, so elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ell
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ell^*(B G)
              seem to be related to flat elliptic objects, i.e. ones such that the operator associated to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo ,
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma,\alpha,\sigma)
              depends on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              only up to homotopy, and is therefore a homomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(\Sigma)\to G
            %p
              I should mention that the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              can be modified by equipping the Riemann surfaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              with chosen spin structures. That is certainly needed to obtain genuine elliptic cohomology.
            %p
              Finally I return to the “contraction property”. This is motivated by the path-integral point of view. If a surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a morphism from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              to itself then the trace of the operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\Sigma)\colon E(S)\to E(S)
              associated to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              must depend only on the
              %em closed
              surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check\Sigma
              obtained by attaching the two boundary pieces of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              to each other. Thus if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\tau
              is the annulus
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{z \in \mathbf{C} \mid |e^{i\tau}|\le |z|\le 1\}
              then
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                        %mi τ
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                        %mrow
                          %mi τ
                          %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check\Sigma_\tau\cong \check\Sigma_{\tau'}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                      %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau'=-1/\tau
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi τ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(\Sigma_\tau)
              is invariant under
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau\mapsto -1/\tau
            %p Brylinski [9] has proposed a similar approach to elliptic cohomology.
            %p Postscript. After giving this talk, I learnt of the work [29] 1 which gives a good account of the Dirac operator on loop space from the path integral point of view.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
            (who introduced them under the name (flat)
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %span.newWikiWord
                  elliptic objects
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/elliptic+objects"} ?
          %ul
            %li#Segal88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Elliptic cohomology (after Landweber–Stong, Ochanine, Witten, and others)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1987-1988__30__187_0"} numdam:SB_1987-1988__30__187_0
          %p These ideas were further developed in
          %ul
            %li#FreedQuinn1991
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+S.+Freed"}> Daniel S. Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Quinn"} Frank Quinn
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chern-Simons Theory with Finite Gauge Group
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9111004"} arXiv:hep-th/9111004
            %li#Quinn95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Quinn"}> Frank Quinn
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on axiomatic topological quantum field theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karen+Uhlenbeck"} Karen Uhlenbeck
                (eds.)
                %em Geometry and Quantum Field Theory
                %strong 1
                (1995) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/pcms/001"}> doi:10.1090/pcms/001
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The theory of HQFTs was developed in
          %ul
            %li#Turaev99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"}> Vladimir Turaev
                ,
                %em Homotopy field theory in dimension 2 and group-algebras
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/9910010"}> arXiv:math.QA/9910010
                )
            %li#Turaev00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"}> Vladimir Turaev
                ,
                %em Homotopy field theory in dimension 3 and crossed group-categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.GT/0005291"}> arXiv:math.GT/0005291
                ).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"} Vladimir Turaev
                (with appendices by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+M%C3%BCger"} Michael Müger
                and
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexis+Virelizier"} Alexis Virelizier
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 10
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ems.press/books/etm/79"} ems:etm/79
            %li#BrightwellTurner00
              %p
                M. Brightwell and P. Turner,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Representations of the homotopy surface category of a simply connected space
            %li#Rodrigues03
              %p
                G. Rodrigues,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy Quantum Field Theories and the Homotopy Cobordism Category in Dimension 1 + 1
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0105018"} arXiv:math.QA/0105018
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T.+Porter"} T. Porter
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Turaev"} V. Turaev
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Formal Homotopy Quantum Field Theories, I: Formal Maps and Crossed
                    = succeed "-algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi C
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0512032"} arXiv:math.QA/0512032
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal Homotopy Quantum Field Theories II: Simplicial Formal Maps
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0512034"} arXiv:math.QA/0512034
          %p
            A treatment of HQFTs that includes some details of the links with TQFTs is given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/HQFT-XMenagerie.pdf", :title => ""} HQFTs meet the Menagerie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
            for a school and workshop in Lisbon, Feb. 2011.
          %p Related ideas are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#MooreSegal06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"}> Greg Moore
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
              %em D-branes and K-theory in 2D topological field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0609042"}> arXiv:hep-th/0609042
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jo%C3%A3o+Faria+Martins"}> João Faria Martins
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Timothy+Porter"} Timothy Porter
                %em A categorification of Quinn’s finite total homotopy TQFT with application to TQFTs and once-extended TQFTs derived from strict omega-groupoids
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.02491"}> arXiv:2301.02491
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christoph+Schweigert"}> Christoph Schweigert
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+Woike"} Lukas Woike
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Extended Homotopy Quantum Field Theories and their Orbifoldization
                %strong 224
                4 (2020) 106213 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08512"}> arXiv:1802.08512
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2019.106213"} doi:10.1016/j.jpaa.2019.106213
          %p
            Understanding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"} circle n-bundles with connection
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+functorial+field+theory"} extended
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+field+theory"} homotopy field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+M%C3%BCller"}> Lukas Müller
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+Woike"} Lukas Woike
              = succeed "," do
                %em Parallel Transport of Higher Flat Gerbes as an Extended Homotopy Quantum Field Theory
              %strong 15
              (2020) 113–142 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.10455"}> arXiv:1802.10455
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s40062-019-00242-3"} doi:10.1007/s40062-019-00242-3
